  "notes": "`high`, because the interview covers gear, tempo and per-track method. **His stated distinction is chopping rather than looping** — he chops drums instead of looping them, then layers over time, *a hi-hat first, shakers and claps added later*. That build order is authored literally: the highest continuous `fillDensity` in this batch because the hat is the foundation the rest is stacked on, a `percs` set of shaker and snap, and a clap on the snare. **The bassline is often written before the drums are finalised**, replayed or filtered, following the chord motion with mids emphasised over sub — so `independent_riff` at 0.50, a register that sits high and narrow-bottomed rather than subterranean, and the chords authored with real motion (`i–iv–VII`, `ii–V–i`) for it to follow. **Time-stretching plus an auto-wah pedal on the source is a documented signature**, giving vowel-like filtered harmony; a model cannot wah, so what stands in for it is `structure: long_arc` with the widest `phraseBars` set here — the chopped bed evolves across the loop rather than restating a 2-bar riff. ~98 BPM is a documented data point and anchors the tempo mode. **Long 24-bar verses** are the researched arrangement fact and are authored as such; beats revisited over years mean the sections are layered rather than one-take, which is why the outro is long.",
